{"type":"assistant","uuid":"32676f62-1b20-48a1-9653-a1516d2ffcb1","session_id":"96bb7398-bccb-417f-961b-8a92ff76631b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"32676f62-1b20-48a1-9653-a1516d2ffcb1","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, now I understand the flow better. Let me trace through what happens when a user watches a video and tries to copy its URL.\n\nThe flow for a video being played:\n1. User navigates to a video (from trending, search, channel, etc.)\n2. `VideoPager.extract()` is called, which for `StreamInfoItem` items:\n   - Gets `id` from `handler.getId(url)` where `handler` is the `streamLinkHandler`\n   - `id` should be a video ID string\n3. `convert(streamInfo, id)` is called, which creates `new YouTubeVideo(id, ...)`\n\nIn the `YouTubePlayer` fragments, when user clicks \"Copy URL\":\n\n\n1. `YouTubeVideo.copyUrl(context)` is called\n2. This calls `SkyTubeApp.copyUrl(context, \"Video URL\", getVideoUrl())`\n3. `getVideoUrl()` returns `\"https://youtu.be/\" + id`\n\nIf `id` is already a full URL like `https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCKvfzCyVQPe2IYg4PkugvWQ`, then `getVideoUrl()` would return the full URL directly instead of constructing a new one, which would explain why the copied URL is the raw channel URL.\n\nThe real question is where `id` gets set to a channel URL instead of a video ID. The `extractor.getId()` from NewPipe's `StreamExtractor` should return a video ID, but there might be an issue with how the video was initially created or parsed—possibly in the YouTube API constructor or when `id` is initially assigned. the YouTube API returns a video ID for the video field. Let me reconsider the bug report more carefully—the user is describing the \"Copy Url\" action from the three-dot menu on a video they received via sharing. Looking at the expected behavior, they want the URL formatted as either `https://youtu.be/7MoipvBda_8` or `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7MoipvBda_8`, but they're getting a malformed URL instead.\n\nThe issue appears to be that when the user copies the URL, `getVideoUrl()` is constructing `https://www.youtube.com/` followed by what looks like a channel URL, suggesting the video ID is being corrupted or replaced with a channel URL somewhere in the process. The `getVideoUrl()` method returns `https://youtu.be/` concatenated with the video ID, so if it's returning a channel URL instead, that means the video ID being used is actually a channel URL—that's where the bug originates.\n\nNow I need to trace where the video ID gets populated to understand how it could end up as a channel URL rather than a valid video identifier. 2. The `copyurl` action is being triggered when it shouldn't be, or it's copying the wrong content.\n\nLooking back at the bug report, the example URL shows a duplicated YouTube domain with a channel path appended, which suggests the `id` variable contains something unexpected. Let me trace through the code to find where `id` might be getting a channel URL instead of a video ID.\n\nIn the `NewPipeService.getDetails()` method, a new `YouTubeVideo` is being created with `extractor.getUploaderUrl()` as the channel URL, but I need to verify what's actually being passed as the video identifier. Looking at the constructor, the first parameter is `id`, the second is `title`, and there's a channel parameter that seems to be getting a `YouTubeChannel` object. I need to trace through how these values are being passed to understand where the issue might be occurring. The `id` field should be a video ID from NewPipe's `extractor.getId()`, but I'm wondering if there's an edge case where it could be something unexpected. Looking at the URL construction issue, the duplicated domain suggests either `id` contains a full URL instead of just an identifier, or there's a prefix being added somewhere else in the code. Let me trace through where the URL might be getting corrupted—possibly in how `getVideoUrl()` constructs the final URL or how the channel URL is being prepended. I'm tracing through the `parse()` method to understand how it handles different link types. For streams, it extracts the ID and canonical URL from the handler factory, then returns a `VideoId` object with those values and a parsed timestamp. For other types, it follows a similar pattern but constructs a generic `ContentId` instead. The key thing I'm noticing is how the URL gets reconstructed using the handler factory's `getUrl()` method with just the extracted ID. The method then routes to appropriate extracters—first trying streams, then channels, then playlists—returning the first successful parse. So I need to understand what `handlerFactory.getId(url)` actually returns, since that's the identifier being extracted from the input URL.\n\nLooking at NewPipe's YouTube extractor specifically, it strips various URL formats (like full watch URLs or shorts links) down to just the raw video ID string. The `getUrl()` method then reconstructs the canonical URL from that ID. But the bug report mentions the URL starts with `https://www.youtube.com/` which seems incomplete—the malformed URL appears to be cut off in the report.\n\nWhen the app copies this URL and it gets opened again, if the video's `id` field somehow contained a full channel URL instead of just a video ID, then `getVideoUrl()` would produce that exact malformed result: `https://youtu.be/https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCKvfzCyVQPe2IYg4PkugvWQ`. So I need to trace where the video `id` field could be getting set to a channel URL in the first place.\n\nI'm checking the various code paths that construct video IDs—`NewPipeService.getDetails()` calls `extractor.getId()` on the `StreamExtractor`, `VideoPager.convert()` gets the ID from the stream link handler, and `VideoPagerWithChannel.convert()` does the same thing. All of these should be returning proper video IDs from NewPipe, but there might be an edge case or initialization path where the ID gets corrupted or set to the wrong value.\n\nLooking at the `getVideoId` method in NewPipeService, I notice it's returning a `VideoId` object that combines the extracted ID with the canonical URL and a parsed timestamp, which suggests the URL is being reconstructed from just the ID string.\n\nBut I'm realizing the bug might not be in the extraction logic itself—the issue could be in how the YouTubeVideo object is serialized or stored. When a video gets deserialized later, the `id` field might not be set correctly, or it could be getting corrupted during the process.\n\nLooking at the constructor that takes individual parameters, it directly assigns `id` to the field without any validation or processing. This seems straightforward, but I should check how `getVideoUrl()` uses this ID to construct the YouTube URL—it should be formatting it as a proper youtu.be link with just the video ID.\n\nThe real issue is that the returned URL contains a full channel URL instead of just the video ID, which means the `id` field is somehow getting set to a complete YouTube URL. I need to trace through the NewPipe StreamExtractor to understand what `extractor.getId()` actually returns in different contexts, since that's likely where the malformed ID is being introduced. The constructor with individual parameters assigns the `id` directly from the `extractor.getId()` call, which should work correctly. But I'm wondering if the issue is in how `getDetails()` constructs the YouTubeChannel object—it's passing `extractor.getUploaderUrl()` as the channel identifier, which might be where the channel URL is incorrectly being stored instead of the video ID. Looking at how videos are instantiated, the `id` parameter comes from the NewPipe extractor's `getId()` method, which should return just the video identifier. But I'm wondering if there's an issue with the YouTube API constructor where the video object might not be properly initialized, or if `getVideoUrl()` is somehow constructing an incorrect URL from the stored ID.\n\nNow I need to trace through the `YouTubePlayer` fragment to see how the `YouTubeVideo` object gets initialized when a video starts playing—whether it's properly set from the start or if there's a point where it could be null or malformed.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
