{"type":"assistant","uuid":"4d0e19cf-5d6e-47a7-aece-4e5ddc7b1d22","session_id":"96bb7398-bccb-417f-961b-8a92ff76631b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"4d0e19cf-5d6e-47a7-aece-4e5ddc7b1d22","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The menu has both `share` and `copyurl` items. The `copyurl` item triggers `youTubeVideo.copyUrl(context)`, which uses `getVideoUrl()`. So it should be a video URL, not a channel URL.\n\nLet me re-read the bug report one more time very carefully:\n\n\"Example invalid url:\nhttps://www.youtube.com/https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCKvfzCyVQPe2IYg4PkugvWQ\"\n\nWait - maybe the user made a mistake in their bug report. Maybe the invalid URL they got was actually `https://youtu.be/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=...` or something similar, but they misremembered or the URL was different than what they reported.\n \nLooking at the structure more carefully, this isn't just a malformed video URL—it's a channel URL with an extra domain prepended to it. The user might have been trying to copy a channel URL from the video's context menu rather than the video URL itself.\n\nActually, let me reconsider this from the user's perspective. When someone shares a video link and then tries to copy it from SkyTube's menu, something goes wrong with the URL construction. The pattern suggests the app is concatenating a base URL with an already-complete URL, creating that duplicated structure.\n\nNow I'm wondering if the issue could be in how the NewPipe extractor itself constructs URLs. The `streamingService.getStreamLHFactory().getUrl(id)` call might be producing an incorrect format, or perhaps the URL construction logic is flawed in a way that causes this duplication when sharing.\n\nBut actually, this method is only used internally for building video stream URLs, not for the \"Copy URL\" feature—that's handled by `getVideoUrl()` instead. Let me step back and focus on what the user is actually reporting: an invalid URL that starts with `https://www.youtube.com/` followed by another YouTube URL, which suggests a duplication or concatenation error somewhere in the URL handling.\n\nThe most likely culprits are either a full URL ending up in the `id` field of a video object, or a channel ID containing a full URL. My `getVideoUrl()` fix would handle the first scenario, and I should apply similar defensive logic to `getChannelUrl()` as well. Let me trace through where `YouTubeChannel` gets instantiated from the stream extractor to see if the channel's URL could be corrupted there.\n\nLooking at the constructor calls, the channel ID is always passed as a raw value from NewPipe extractors—it's never already a full URL. So the `YouTubeVideo` constructor shouldn't be the source of the problem either.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the user's description about \"a video you receive to share\"—they might be talking about sharing videos within the app rather than receiving them from outside sources. Or perhaps the issue isn't with the `id` field itself, but with how the URL is being constructed or processed downstream.\n\nLet me trace through what could cause a duplicated URL pattern. If the user copies the video URL and then someone else pastes it back into the app to play it, the app would need to parse that URL again. I'm checking where the URL gets constructed in `YouTubeVideo.getVideoUrl()` and whether the `id` field could somehow end up containing a full YouTube URL instead of just a video identifier. If that happens, the URL construction would produce something like `https://youtu.be/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ`, which would be malformed.\n\nBut the actual reported URL has `youtube.com` in it, not `youtu.be`, so the duplication pattern doesn't match what `getVideoUrl()` produces. Looking at the reported URL more carefully, it appears to be a channel URL with the domain duplicated: `youtube.com` followed by another `youtube.com/channel/...`. This suggests the duplication is happening elsewhere in the code, not in the video URL construction method.\n\nNow I'm wondering if the user might have been trying to copy the channel URL from the video player's context menu instead. Looking at the menu structure, there's a channel-related section that includes options like opening the channel, subscribing, and blocking—maybe one of these operations is generating the duplicated URL.\n\nBut examining the code flow more carefully, the \"Copy URL\" button directly calls `copyUrl` rather than constructing a URL by concatenating the base URL with the channel's identifier. So even if the channel ID were somehow a full URL, it wouldn't produce the duplication the user reported. The menu is definitely showing the channel's context, but the copy operation itself doesn't seem to be the source of the problem. The menu item is labeled \"Open Channel\" though, not \"Copy URL\", so I'm realizing the user might actually be describing a channel URL being copied rather than a video URL. But looking at their reproduction steps—\"Go to any youtube video, Click on the three points, Click Copy Url, Paste the url\"—this seems like it should be producing a video URL, not a channel URL.\n\nMaybe the real issue is that `getVideoUrl()` constructs an invalid video URL, and when they paste it elsewhere, the service interprets it incorrectly or shows a related channel instead. Looking at the actual URL structure they're seeing, it's not even a video URL at all—it's malformed and points to a channel. This suggests the wrong URL source is being used when \"Copy URL\" executes. Looking at the `parse` method more carefully, when it's called with STREAM type, it creates a `VideoId` that contains both the video ID and a canonical URL. If the video ID is somehow a channel URL instead, the `getUrl()` method would construct a malformed canonical URL with two YouTube domains concatenated. This could happen if the parsing logic receives a channel URL where it expects a video URL, causing both the ID and the constructed canonical URL to be incorrect. I'm seeing the issue now—the `parse` method is actually checking against `StreamingService.LinkType.STREAM`, not CHANNEL, so the bug report must be showing a different problem. Let me focus on what's actually happening: the invalid URL being returned is a channel URL rather than a video URL, which suggests the code is incorrectly constructing or fetching a channel link when it should be returning video metadata. I think the real issue is that `id` is being set to a full YouTube URL instead of just the video identifier. When `getVideoUrl()` then concatenates `https://youtu.be/` with that full URL, it creates the malformed result I'm seeing. I can fix this by extracting just the video ID from the URL in `getVideoUrl()` and using that for the copy operation instead.\n\nBut looking back at the agent's analysis, they claim `id` should always be a raw video ID and there's no code path where a channel URL gets assigned to it. I've already verified that all the constructors set `id` to raw video IDs from various sources. However, I notice the `CardData` class has a setter method that could theoretically set `id` to any string, and deserialization through `Serializable` might be where the problem occurs.\n\nActually, deserialization should just restore whatever the constructor set in the first place, so that's not it either. I'm convinced my fix to `getVideoUrl()` handles edge cases properly and follows good defensive programming practices. I should also apply the same defensive approach to `getChannelUrl()` in `YouTubeChannel.java` to be thorough.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the actual bug report more carefully—the user mentions \"a video you receive to share,\" which means the video likely comes from an external intent when someone shares a YouTube link to SkyTube. I need to trace through what happens when that intent arrives at the main activity to see where the URL duplication might be happening. When `openContent` receives a stream, it calls `YouTubePlayer.launch(content, context)` which creates an intent to view the video by parsing the `ContentId`'s canonical URL. For video streams, this canonical URL gets constructed in the `parse` method when extracting the video ID from the handler factory.\n\nBut I'm realizing the canonical URL is built using `streamingService.getStreamLHFactory().getUrl(id)` where `id` is extracted from the original URL—this seems like it could be where the duplication is happening, since it's reconstructing the URL rather than preserving what was originally shared.\n\nLooking at the actual bug report, the user mentions the video URL from sharing is invalid and contains just the channel, with the example showing a malformed URL that starts with the channel base. The issue seems to be that when receiving a shared video, the URL construction is producing something that points to a channel instead of the actual video.\n\nI think my `getVideoUrl()` fix should handle this edge case, so I'll keep it as is. Now I should check `getChannelUrl()` to see if it needs the same kind of fix.\n\nLooking at the `YoutubeChannelLinkHandlerFactory`, if `getId()` returns a full URL instead of just the channel identifier, the URL construction would break. For example, if `id` is \"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCxxx\", then `getUrl(id)` would concatenate it with the base, producing \"https://www.youtube.com/https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCxxx\" — which matches the malformed URL in the bug report exactly. So the root cause is that `getChannelUrl()` is passing a full URL to the factory method when it should only pass the channel ID. I can fix this by extracting just the ID portion from the channel's `getId()` method before constructing the URL, falling back to the full ID if parsing fails.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
